# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Runtime
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Set NODE_ENV=production on Render: the server then serves dist/ and never
# starts the Vite development middleware. Without it, `npm start` runs Vite in
# middleware mode (development only).
# NODE_ENV="production"

# APP_URL: public origin used to build customer invitation links, e.g.
# https://ultraviolet-copilot.onrender.com . Must be https in production, and
# must not point at the AI Studio `ais-dev-*` preview host (Google sign-in).
APP_URL="MY_APP_URL"

# ULTRAVOX API KEYS (server-side only; never sent to browsers).
#
# Option A (backward compatible, single key):
# ULTRAVOX_API_KEY="<your Ultravox API key>"
#
# Option B (recommended, multiple keys for bounded failover): a JSON array of
# non-secret ids plus server-side keys. Ids appear in operator diagnostics; keys
# never do. On Render, paste the one-line JSON value into the dashboard env var
# (the file is not committed with real values).
# ULTRAVOX_API_KEYS='[{"id":"primary","key":"<key 1>"},{"id":"backup","key":"<key 2>"}]'
#
# A compact list form also works:
# ULTRAVOX_API_KEYS="primary=<key 1>,backup=<key 2>"
#
# Rules: ids are 1-32 chars of letters/digits/._- and unique; keys are trimmed,
# duplicates and placeholders such as MY_ULTRAVOX_API_KEY are rejected; at most
# 10 keys are read. If BOTH variables are set, ULTRAVOX_API_KEYS wins and the
# legacy variable is ignored (with a safe warning). If ULTRAVOX_API_KEYS is
# present but invalid, a valid ULTRAVOX_API_KEY is used as a fallback.
#
# Key rotation is bounded and evidence-based: 401/403 (without quota wording),
# invalid requests, unknown errors and 5xx do NOT cycle keys. Quota exhaustion
# and rate limits do, within ULTRAVOX_MAX_KEY_ATTEMPTS. Ultravox documents
# account-level rate/concurrency limits, so multiple keys help only when they
# belong to separate accounts/projects; see docs/deployment.md.
# Cooldowns are in-process only (lost on restart/redeploy).
# ULTRAVOX_MAX_KEY_ATTEMPTS="3"
# ULTRAVOX_KEY_QUOTA_COOLDOWN_SECONDS="3600"
# ULTRAVOX_KEY_RATE_LIMIT_COOLDOWN_SECONDS="30"
# ULTRAVOX_KEY_AUTH_COOLDOWN_SECONDS="300"

# ULTRAVOX_LANGUAGE_HINT: optional BCP47 hint for Ultravox speech recognition
# and synthesis, e.g. "es-MX". It is a hint, not detection, and applies to the
# whole call. Leave it unset for multilingual / code-switching customers: the
# AI is instructed to mirror the customer's language and no language is forced.
# ULTRAVOX_LANGUAGE_HINT="es-MX"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Peer-to-peer call (salesperson <-> customer) ICE configuration
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STUN alone only works when both devices can reach each other directly. When
# either side is behind a symmetric / carrier-grade NAT (a phone on mobile data)
# no direct path exists and media never flows. TURN is the only fix.
#
# TURN_URLS: comma-separated turn:/turns: URLs.
# TURN_URLS="turn:turn.example.com:3478?transport=udp,turns:turn.example.com:5349?transport=tcp"
#
# TURN authentication: prefer a shared secret (coturn `use-auth-secret`, i.e.
# the TURN REST API). The server mints short-lived credentials per session and
# never sends the secret to browsers.
# TURN_SECRET="long-random-shared-secret"
# TURN_TTL_SECONDS="3600"
#
# Static credentials are a fallback (not recommended: the password reaches every
# authorized browser).
# TURN_USERNAME="turn-user"
# TURN_CREDENTIAL="turn-password"
#
# Managed providers can issue short-lived credentials from a server-side
# endpoint (Twilio Network Traversal, Metered, or your own proxy). The response
# must be an array of {urls|url, username, credential} or {iceServers:[...]}.
# The API key/Authorization header stays on the server and is never logged.
# TURN_CREDENTIALS_URL="https://your-provider.example.com/turn-credentials"
# TURN_CREDENTIALS_AUTH="Bearer <server-side-provider-key>"
#
# rstream managed STUN/TURN (temporary, project-scoped credentials).
# The token is a server-side secret: it is never sent to browsers or logs. The
# server calls the documented Control plane route on demand:
#   POST https://rstream.io/api/projects/tunnels/resolve/<endpoint>/turn-server/credentials
#   Authorization: Bearer <token>, body {"ttlSeconds": 600}, response
#   { username, credential, urls[], ttl }.
# Set exactly one project selector (endpoint preferred). Leave TURN_URLS unset
# unless you also want the static/ephemeral TURN fallback.
# RSTREAM_AUTHENTICATION_TOKEN="<rstream access token>"
# RSTREAM_PROJECT_ENDPOINT="<rstream project endpoint>"
# RSTREAM_PROJECT_ID="<rstream project id>"
# RSTREAM_API_BASE_URL="https://rstream.io"
#
# Static rstream TURN JSON credentials (temporary testing; no API token needed).
# Paste username/credential/urls from your rstream TURN JSON response. URLs are
# comma-separated. THESE EXPIRE: the server does not renew them, and after
# RSTREAM_TURN_TTL_SECONDS (default 600, max 3600) they are withheld from ICE
# responses until you replace them with fresh values and redeploy.
# The API flow above stays preferred when RSTREAM_AUTHENTICATION_TOKEN and a
# project identifier are also configured.
# RSTREAM_TURN_USERNAME="<json username>"
# RSTREAM_TURN_CREDENTIAL="<json credential>"
# RSTREAM_TURN_URLS="turn:host:3478?transport=udp,turns:host:5349?transport=tcp"
# RSTREAM_TURN_TTL_SECONDS="600"
#
# ExpressTURN free tier (operator-entered, temporary testing).
#
# The free plan provides a TURN server URL, a username and a password. It does
# NOT provide a Secret Key (shared-secret/HMAC auth is Premium-only), so there is
# no secret key to configure anywhere.
#
# No environment variable is required. Open https://<service>/admin/turn: the
# first visit asks you to create a setup password (at least 12 characters using
# three character classes). Only a scrypt hash of that password is kept, in
# server memory. Later visits sign in with it and then enter the three
# ExpressTURN values. TURN passwords are never returned to the browser after
# saving.
#
# Both the setup password and the TURN credentials are process-local: a restart
# or redeploy clears them and the page returns to first-time setup, where you
# create a new setup password and re-enter the ExpressTURN values. Nothing is
# written to disk, a database or Git.
#
# ADMIN_PASSWORD is no longer read. If an older deployment still sets it, it is
# ignored; remove it. No SETTINGS_STORE_PATH, persistent disk, external
# database, Render Shell or ExpressTURN Secret Key is used.
# STUN_URLS="stun:stun.l.google.com:19302,stun:stun1.l.google.com:19302"
#
# ICE_TRANSPORT_POLICY: "all" (default) or "relay". Relay-only is a
# verification mode: it fails fast (HTTP 503 on the ICE endpoint) when no usable
# TURN relay is configured, instead of silently falling back to STUN.
# ICE_TRANSPORT_POLICY="all"

# MAX_SESSIONS: in-memory session cap (default 500). Sessions are not persisted;
# a restart destroys them. See docs/prototype2-architecture.md.
# MAX_SESSIONS="500"
